direction: right

title: |md
  ## struct_ops: BPF replaces a kernel vtable
| {near: top-center; shape: text}

before: "Before struct_ops" {
  style.fill: "#fee2e2"
  b_body: |md
    A subsystem has a vtable in C:
    ```c
    struct tcp_congestion_ops bbr = {
        .name = "bbr",
        .init = bbr_init,
        .cong_avoid = bbr_main,
        ...
    };
    tcp_register_congestion_control(&bbr);
    ```

    To add a new CC algorithm:
    - write C code in tree
    - compile as kernel module
    - load .ko
    - hope your code doesn't crash
  |
}

after: "With struct_ops" {
  style.fill: "#dcfce7"
  a_body: |md
    A BPF program defines the vtable:
    ```c
    struct tcp_congestion_ops bbr_bpf = {
        .name = "bbr_bpf",
        .init = (void *)bbr_init_bpf,
        .cong_avoid = (void *)bbr_main_bpf,
    };
    SEC(".struct_ops") struct tcp_congestion_ops
        bbr_bpf;
    ```
    Each function is a BPF program.

    Loading registers it via
    `tcp_register_congestion_control`.
    Userspace selects with `setsockopt`.
  |
}

powers: |md
  **Powers two big features:**
  - **TCP Congestion Control** in BPF
  - **sched_ext** — BPF schedulers (Day 25+)

  Anywhere the kernel has a `struct ops`
  with function pointers, struct_ops can
  let BPF supply implementations.
| {shape: callout; style.fill: "#fef3c7"}

before -> after
after -> powers
